speechtext:
I am afraid that is rather a large contract for me at this hour of the night. I wish to submit, Mr. Chairman, that there is no clear idea present before the committee at this moment as to how far the Bank Act may be changed at any time. The statement has been made that it can be amended at any time. Now, how far is that true? If it is true that fundamental changes may be made at any time, if it is true that the significance of granting a ten-year charter is practically nothing at all, then of course there is no practical difference between the granting of charters for one year and the granting of charters for ten years, but I do submit that unless we are going to interpret the granting of ten-year charters differently from what we have understood in the past, it is entirely unwise and improper under present conditions, and in view of all these questions which have been presented to the committee and which the country has to solve in some way or other in the near future-it is entirely unwise, I say, to crystallize in any way the financial system by granting ten-year charters.
This afternoon, Mr. Chairman, an effort was made to move the following amendment which I would like to put on Hansard in connection with the Merchants Bank affair:
Whereas the select standing committee on Banking and Commerce decided by resolution not to hear evidence dealing with the collapse of the Merchants Bank, and its subsequent amalgamation or merger with the Bank of Montreal;
